@chapter Synopsis

ffhls -i @file{input} -unix_socket @file{path} [@var{options}]

@chapter Description
@c man begin DESCRIPTION

@command{ffhls} binds to a local UNIX domain socket and speaks just enough
HTTP to serve an HLS (HTTP Live Streaming) presentation to a web browser media
engine. Safari on iOS, iPadOS and macOS plays the stream natively; Chrome and
Firefox can play it through a Media Source Extensions player such as
@uref{https://github.com/video-dev/hls.js, hls.js}.

The input media is transcoded to H.264/AAC MPEG-TS segments entirely in memory
--- nothing is written to disk. Segments are produced @emph{on demand} when the
client requests them, and a small, adaptive read-ahead window pre-buffers a few
upcoming segments. The amount of media that is pre-buffered is derived from the
rate at which the client pulls segments, so a client playing back at up to 4x
(which drains its buffer faster and therefore requests segments more often) is
kept fed without wastefully transcoding the whole file ahead of time.

Because the media playlist is a VOD playlist that lists every segment, and each
segment begins with a forced IDR frame, the client may seek to any position;
@command{ffhls} simply transcodes the requested segment.

@chapter HTTP endpoints
@c man begin ENDPOINTS

@table @option
@item /
A minimal HTML player page (native HLS, or hls.js where supported).
@item /master.m3u8
The HLS master playlist.
@item /stream.m3u8
The HLS media (VOD) playlist listing every segment.
@item /seg-@var{N}.ts
MPEG-TS segment number @var{N}, transcoded on demand. @code{Range} requests are
supported.
@end table

@chapter Options
@c man begin OPTIONS

@table @option
@item -i @var{input}
Input media file or URL (required).
@item -unix_socket @var{path}
Path of the UNIX domain socket to bind and listen on (required). Aliases:
@option{-unix}, @option{-listen}.
@item -seg_duration @var{seconds}
Target segment duration. Default 4.
@item -buffer_segments @var{n}
Upper bound on the adaptive read-ahead window, in segments. Default 6.
@item -max_memory @var{mb}
In-memory segment cache cap, in mebibytes. Default 96.
@item -lookahead @var{seconds}
Amount of media time the read-ahead window tries to keep buffered. Default 8.
@item -vb @var{bps}
Video bitrate. Default 2000000.
@item -ab @var{bps}
Audio bitrate. Default 128000.
@item -vcodec @var{name}
Override the H.264 video encoder (default tries @code{libx264} and other H.264
encoders).
@end table

@chapter Examples
@c man begin EXAMPLES

Serve a file and fetch the master playlist with @command{curl}:
@example
ffhls -i movie.mkv -unix_socket /tmp/hls.sock &
curl --unix-socket /tmp/hls.sock http://localhost/master.m3u8
@end example

A browser cannot open a UNIX socket directly; put a forwarder in front of it,
for example with @command{socat}:
@example
socat TCP-LISTEN:8080,reuseaddr,fork UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/hls.sock
@end example
then open @code{http://localhost:8080/} in the browser.

@c man end

